#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059476 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059648 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8594bbfe-b098-5aad-9b9e-29997798c73b" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Negative Angles in the Coordinate Plane With the Unit Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "9d157fc4-9b82-5430-8622-dd5bb155b123" :question/math-academy-id "e-3158" :question/problem "Construct a unit circle in the coordinate plane that shows a central angle of $-75^\\circ$ in the standard position." :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, and its vertex is at the origin.\n\n- It rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise, and a negative angle is measured clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/c0/c039d0cdc7d0f127cdca06652328e84a458faaeedd4d6e610f452b0e93ba5c21.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059477 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059649 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae40b03b-8dd7-56f6-95a1-96fd20de50bb" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Negative Angles in Radians in the Coordinate Plane With the Unit Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "2026745e-59ca-5365-82fc-2683b026fac5" :question/math-academy-id "e-3159" :question/problem "Construct a unit circle in the coordinate plane with a central angle of $-\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ in the standard position." :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\dfrac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{2\\pi} 3 \\right) \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac {180^\\circ} {\\pi}\\right) = \n\t-120^\\circ.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $-\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ is equivalent to $-120^\\circ.$\n\nThen, recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis and its vertex is at the origin.\n\n- A positive angle has a rotation that is measured counter-clockwise, and a negative angle is measured clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/f9/f920f36cc5dcbbfaa94f901a21bf5c19f4dc6846542d3d3c00ccbd5631a6362f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059478 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059650 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e67c1979-cee7-5eab-81d5-b185976b88b7" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Negative Angles in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "4a601091-a7ee-5733-b17d-d01acced88b3" :question/math-academy-id "e-3160" :question/problem "Construct the angle $-225^\\circ$ in the coordinate plane in the usual way." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that:\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis, and its vertex lies at the origin.\n\n- It rotates such that a positive angle is measured counter-clockwise, and a negative angle is measured clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/33/3304f581480780d7139bac50ded646f78a8c8c344c0184ad81955bcc83acbe72.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535923 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059648 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8594bbfe-b098-5aad-9b9e-29997798c73b" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Negative Angles in the Coordinate Plane With the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01d95c06-49fb-5b34-b3bb-fbc0e9f3742e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/42/42453c7bd34a8cb85dfcb3bc638a148052f685e832987f4e901f308514b731c6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535438} {:answer/id #uuid "1b1140ca-4a8c-5188-b173-777a2e6c847e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592