mage} :answer/value "images/1e/1e9e8c8b4fa25a975cd718571c811b4dd24e6332df9b696301db39af39ab6a03.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058677} {:answer/id #uuid "0c7a3f58-85c6-516c-ba95-2e5b5fa7d602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/98/98c0ebe3711145f3919cba4ce44b4a800eab948886ff5d87eb974194e37b0e66.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058678} {:answer/id #uuid "1c575ce2-1541-5cea-b375-08912505ef6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a3/a3106bc6788215147adfed66ac038523a73191a9b0182f35089bd1d7b2f87787.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c575ce2-1541-5cea-b375-08912505ef6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a3/a3106bc6788215147adfed66ac038523a73191a9b0182f35089bd1d7b2f87787.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058679} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7570d1a-a618-5ed6-9675-a5192d884029" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c67cd7a-c282-58e6-83e7-15a2075597f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-65154" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams shows the unit circle in the coordinate plane with a central angle of $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ in the standard position?" :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot (\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is equivalent to $-{45}^{∘}.$\n\nThen, recall that:\n\n- A unit circle has a radius of $1$ and is centered at the origin in the coordinate plane.\n\n- An angle in the standard position has its initial side on the positive $x$-axis and its vertex is at the origin.\n\n- A positive angle has a rotation that is measured counter-clockwise, and a negative angle is measured clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct diagram is as follows:\n\n![](images/a3/a3106bc6788215147adfed66ac038523a73191a9b0182f35089bd1d7b2f87787.png)"}]}
